=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
The Kath2 network is fully operational. No router has died and no connectivity
fault exists. A systematic inspection of all three routers (router0, router1,
router2) shows every relevant subsystem is healthy: all physical interfaces are
UP with correctly assigned IP addresses, all FRR daemons (watchfrr, zebra,
mgmtd, ripd, staticd) are running, RIP v2 has converged fully on every router
with complete routing tables covering all seven subnets
(192.168.1–4.0/24, 1.0.0.0/8, 128.168.0.0/16, 10.0.0.0/8, 20.0.0.0/8), IP
forwarding is enabled, and no iptables rules are present on any router. Direct
ping tests from multiple Office LAN hosts (office_pc, enquiry) to the Server
Room (pc1, 1.0.0.5) and the Internet Lab (pc2–pc5, 128.168.0.x) all succeed
with 0% packet loss, as does a full lab-wide reachability sweep (12/12 pairs
OK). The helpdesk ticket's assertion that "one of the routers in the middle has
died" is factually incorrect: router1, which sits between router0 and router2
and serves as the Office LAN gateway, is completely operational. The root cause
of the ticket is a false premise — the reported symptom does not correspond to
any real fault present in the network. No corrective action is required; the
on-call engineer should close this ticket as a false alarm and advise the user
to re-test connectivity directly before escalating.
=== END ===
